 What is a Secret?

 A Secret in Kubernetes is used to store sensitive information such as:

 Passwords

 API keys

 Tokens

 Certificates


 Unlike ConfigMap, Secrets are meant for confidential data 


Simple Definition

Secret = Secure storage for sensitive data in Kubernetes


Why Use Secrets?
	
	Avoid hardcoding credentials in code

	Secure handling of sensitive data

	Easy updates without rebuilding images

	Better access control via RBAC


Types of Secrets

Type																	Use Case

Opaque (default)										Generic key-value

kubernetes.io/dockerconfigjson			Docker registry auth

kubernetes.io/tls										TLS certs (SSL)


Example 1: Create Secret (YAML)

Values must be Base64 encoded

echo -n "admin" | base64

echo -n "password123" | base64



Example 


Secret YAML

apiVersion: v1

kind: Secret

metadata:
  name: db-secret
  namespace: dev 
  
type: Opaque
data:
  username: YWRtaW4=
  password: cGFzc3dvcmQxMjM=



Example 4: Create Secret via Command

kubectl create secret generic db-secret \
  --from-literal=username=admin \
  --from-literal=password=password123

Create Secret

kubectl apply -f secret.yaml


Get Secrets

kubectl get secrets

Describe Secret

kubectl describe secret db-secret


View Secret (YAML)

kubectl get secret db-secret -o yaml


Decode Secret

kubectl get secret db-secret -o jsonpath="{.data.username}" | base64 --decode


Delete Secret

kubectl delete secret db-secret


Check inside Pod

kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- env | grep DB_USER


kubectl exec -it <pod-name> -- ls /etc/secret


Secret vs ConfigMap


Feature				Secret 		ConfigMap 

Data				Sensitive	Non-sensitive

Encoding			Base64		Plain text

Use case			Passwords	Config values


